00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.160
Challenges with Python package documentation.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.160
Here's one.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.160
It's often the first thing people look at before they commit to actually using the software.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:20.160
So it might be a deciding factor as to whether they use your software.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.160
Okay.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:26.160
So documentation, it has to be good to keep existing users engaged.

00:05:26.279 --> 00:05:31.279
Otherwise, people may turn to alternatives with better docs.

00:05:31.279 --> 00:05:35.279
More lately, an agent might read the docs first.

00:05:35.279 --> 00:05:41.279
So we probably should keep in mind that, you know, a doc site might be better suited for

00:05:41.279 --> 00:05:42.279
agents.

00:05:42.279 --> 00:05:47.279
So I got some responses for people that do docs.

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:49.279
Three cheese points.

00:05:49.279 --> 00:05:52.279
One is, like, design for a good initial impression.

00:05:52.399 --> 00:05:57.399
Organization, presence of reference and guides should be there.

00:05:57.399 --> 00:06:04.399
And then to keep the users engaged, we need good authoring tools and use those good authoring

00:06:04.399 --> 00:06:07.399
tools for the reference and guides.

00:06:07.399 --> 00:06:12.399
And finally, we need things suited for agents, like pages and markdown form, lms.txt, and

00:06:12.399 --> 00:06:16.399
maybe even documenting skills and having skills available.

00:06:16.399 --> 00:06:17.399
Okay.

00:06:17.399 --> 00:06:21.399
