00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:05.040
just give you an inkling of what Term Show is.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.040
So, usually, like I said,

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:09.040
CLI and TUI demos

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:11.040
usually mean a GIF or a video.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.040
They can be huge, sometimes blurry

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:15.040
because the text is not really well recorded

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:17.040
or you're recording at a low bitrate.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.040
You often can't

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:21.040
select the text and you often

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:23.040
can't even pause the playback.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.040
So, you want to rewind

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:27.040
a bit, but you have to wait

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:29.040
until the next go around if it's a GIF.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:31.040
Very frustrating.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.040
So, I built a thing where

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.040
you just record your terminal activity,

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:37.040
you get a file, and then

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:39.040
there's also a paired YAML file.

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:41.040
This is for a configuration and presentation,

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:43.040
essentially, like the way you want to show it.

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:45.040
And, as you imagine,

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:47.040
it can get pretty crazy editing

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.040
YAML file to make it just right.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:51.040
So, what I did was also I created

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:53.040
an editor for this. It's a

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:55.040
browser-based editor,

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:57.040
and it allows you...

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:59.040
It's almost like a little final cut for terminal

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.040
presentations, and I'll show you that in the

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:03.040
demo pretty soon.

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:05.040
Okay.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:07.040
This is great, but what

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:09.040
users, like viewers, see

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:11.040
is sharp

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:13.040
SVG animation playback. It's got

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:15.040
its own full-featured player.

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:17.040
It's a lot like a video player.

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:19.040
And chapters, annotations,

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:21.040
you can have those in there, and you can

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.040
teach with that. You have all the tools

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:25.040
to demonstrate what your

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:27.040
CLI does and show

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:29.040
workflows.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:31.040
So, I'm going to show that to you right now.

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:33.040
Okay.

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:35.040
So, I've got a tab open right here called Term Show

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.040
Editor, and here's a video I made a while

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:39.040
back. And, essentially, you can scrub around

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:41.040
and see almost like the live

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:43.040
video of your recording.

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:45.040
And you can just add

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.040
chapter marks. I can just double-click here, add a

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.040
chapter mark, and I can label it

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.040
new chapter, for instance.

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:53.040
And

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:55.040
it's kind of cool. Now I can scrub around and see I'm in

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.040
new chapter up here.

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:59.040
Very nice. You can always just hit play

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:01.040
to play back, which is kind of nice. You sort of

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:03.040
see it live. This is like an emulation of the final

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:05.040
thing.

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:07.040
You can add things like cuts. Say you don't want

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.040
to see a bunch of the video, just double-click here,

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:11.040
change the width of this, and you

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.040
can cut out some of the

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:15.040
extra dead space if you want.

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:17.040
So, if I start playing back, it'll just jump over

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:19.040
that, which is cool,

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:21.040
because sometimes you just have a lot of, you just stutter

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:23.040
over certain things, and

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:25.040
your terminal,

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:27.040
your demonstration is a bit overlong.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.040
You might just want to cut bits out.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:31.040
We also have something called

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.040
snippets. What that does

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:35.040
is it shows you in the

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:37.040
top right corner,

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:39.040
right here, you can have a little button, and you

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.040
can have text that's copyable. Say, for

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:43.040
instance, the command. You can

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:45.040
just have users click that, and they can

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:47.040
grab that command and put it in their terminal

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:49.040
if they need it. And it's shown at the right

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:51.040
time and in the right place up here.

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:53.040
So, really cool. And there's

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:55.040
also other things I'm not going to show you, like highlights.

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:57.040
You can sort of annotate pieces of

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:59.040
the actual terminal

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:01.040
characters and such.

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:03.040
The boxes around things.

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:05.040
The thing I want to show you also is, this is nice, but

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:07.040
what if you wanted to

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:09.040
just see it, how it would look on a page?

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:11.040
There's a preview button here,

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:13.040
and what it does is it just goes back,

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:15.040
of course, and does a render.

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:17.040
And this is how it actually looks inside the player.

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.040
And this is actually good, because I get to show you the

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:21.040
player, which is kind of neat. You can pause it,

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:23.040
scrub around, go to the chapter

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:25.040
marker here, click here, and you see

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.040
new chapter I just made. And this is

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:29.040
essentially an unsaved state, so before you even

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:31.040
commit it to save, you can just look at it

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.040
right here and go to the end.

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.040
You can change the player speed in case you like to go

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.040
really fast.

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:39.040
But this is kind of nice. Before you even

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.040
commit to rendering it,

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:43.040
you can make sure it's just right.

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:45.040
So that, in a nutshell, is

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:47.040
Term Show and the Term Show Editor.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:49.040
I'll go back to my slides.

00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:53.040
All right.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:55.040
Okay, so

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:57.040
it doesn't end there in terms of features.

00:27:57.040 --> 00:27:59.040
There's lots of features.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.040
A big one I wanted,

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:03.040
I'm really into tables.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:05.040
I have this project called Great Tables,

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:07.040
so definitely tables is kind of

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:09.040
my bag. But I think it's

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:11.040
useful to have that in documentation sometimes,

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.040
without importing Great Tables

00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:15.040
or some other table library.

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:17.040
So I did something called

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:19.040
Table Preview and Table Explorer. Two different

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:21.040
things, but they're kind of

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:23.040
cut from the same cloth.

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.040
Table Preview is kind of like a simple

00:28:25.040 --> 00:28:27.040
static table. It gives

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:29.040
you a small preview of the data, usually

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:31.040
like

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.040
ten rows, or it could be the

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:35.040
first, fifth, five rows, and the last five

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:37.040
rows. And it shows you

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:39.040
things like column types,

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.040
and it's pretty smart about being

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.040
wide, but not too wide, in terms of column

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:45.040
width controls.

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:47.040
And the really good thing about it also is that

00:28:47.040 --> 00:28:49.040
you can import from many different formats,

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.040
like we're talking about DataFrames,

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:53.040
CSV files, JSONL, Feather

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:55.040
files, Parquet.

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:57.040
It's really great in terms of importing.

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:01.040
Okay. If you need a table,

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:03.040
it's a bit more interactive, full-fledged.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:05.040
Table Explorer might be what you need.

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:07.040
So in this case, you have something

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:09.040
that's JavaScript-enabled.

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:11.040
It has all the things you might expect, like sortable

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:13.040
columns. You can sort by multiple columns.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.040
It has buttons for downloading

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:17.040
the filtered and sorted data,

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:19.040
and a means to copy as

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:21.040
CSV text. And the filtering interface,

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:23.040
I'll show you how it looks when you actually have

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:25.040
some filters in place. You just click

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:27.040
the Add Filter button, and it presents

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:29.040
to you a small interface, which

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.040
depending on the column type,

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:35.040
will present different options for filtering.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:37.040
And then they just appear as these tokens

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:39.040
on the field right there, which you can

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:41.040
remove, or you can add

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:43.040
more. So it's really

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:45.040
quite nice.

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:47.040
The lightbox thing is

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:49.040
basically born from frustration

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:51.040
of showing a few images,

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:53.040
and I figure it's a nice thing to have

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:55.040
in documentation. You never know when you'll need it

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:57.040
until you need it.

00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:59.040
So what

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:01.040
it does, basically, you click

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:03.040
on an image, it brings you in this view, and

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.040
it just makes it large, essentially.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:07.040
And if you have a gallery, and

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:09.040
you can have a gallery, you can just use the arrow keys

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:11.040
to page through the gallery.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:13.040
And you can have dark mode,

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.040
image swap, so you might have

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:17.040
for instance, images for light

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:19.040
and for dark mode, like a pair.

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:21.040
You can have before and after

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:23.040
comparisons, which basically presents to you

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:25.040
this slider.

00:30:25.040 --> 00:30:27.040
And another cool thing, which I'm not really showing here, is annotations.

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:29.040
You can have certain regions of an image

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:31.040
annotated, having dots and

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:33.040
callouts.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:37.040
It's really small on the top right, but this is

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:39.040
zoomed in on the controls.

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:41.040
And we offer things like, of course, zoom,

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:43.040
the ability to copy image.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:45.040
You can download the image, and the really cool thing is

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:47.040
getting a link to the image. So basically the link

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:49.040
will just mean, if you provide that link to someone else

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.040
and they follow it, it'll bring that

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:53.040
person to exactly this lightbox

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:55.040
view.

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:59.040
Okay, wrapping up.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:01.040
So I want to conclude with a few points.

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:03.040
Great docs. It tries to be nice by default,

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:05.040
but powerful when you want more.

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:07.040
I kind of believe there should be a tradeoff

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.040
between an easy setup and having

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:11.040
depth control. So

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.040
great docs has sort of been designed to give you both.

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:17.040
Just a thought, I think we should treat documentation

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:19.040
as a product, put a lot of weight

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:21.040
on it. It's really what

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:23.040
people and agents judge your

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:25.040
project on before they really commit to using it.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:27.040
So it deserves some design

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.040
depth and care.

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:31.040
Okay, this is a bit of

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:33.040
a spicy statement, but I'm going to say it anyway.

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:35.040
So your documentation is now kind of like an API

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:37.040
for agents as much as people.

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:39.040
So when an agent is

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:41.040
sometimes their first reader,

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:43.040
often, you might say, things like

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:45.040
skills, mcp docs, lms.txt,

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:47.040
they're kind of like table stakes

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:49.040
now. They stop being extras and become

00:31:49.040 --> 00:31:51.040
required, you might say.

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:55.040
Great docs, they

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:57.040
teach you. They don't just describe things.

00:31:57.040 --> 00:31:59.040
So if you have things like runnable examples,

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:01.040
callouts, and guides, which are

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.040
top level with your reference,

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:05.040
you might even have

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:07.040
terminal

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:09.040
presentations, they're all there to

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:11.040
actually teach your users.

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:15.040
And being good on mobile,

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:17.040
it shouldn't be an afterthought. Docs should be

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:19.040
just as good on a phone as they are on a desktop

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:21.040
for human readers.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:23.040
This is why things

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:25.040
like parameter tables, sometimes they're

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:27.040
put in tables and you get

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:29.040
a very long parameter on the left side

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:31.040
and description that wraps over multiple lines

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:33.040
on the right.

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:35.040
Probably shouldn't do that on mobile.

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.040
There's some thought there to make it

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:39.040
read better and have better

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:41.040
architecture

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:43.040
of the information

00:32:43.040 --> 00:32:45.040
constrained with devices.

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.040
So I want to say thanks.

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:51.040
Check out Great Docs. It's

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:53.040
available in GitHub under

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:55.040
positivegreatdocs.

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.040
And if you do try it out, let me know how it goes. I'm really interested

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:59.040
in people that do try it out, and I

00:32:59.040 --> 00:33:01.040
really want to help people if there's

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:03.040
issues at all.

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:05.040
Thank you.

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:07.040
Thank you so much, Rich, and thank you for the

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:09.040
tour of Great Docs.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:11.040
Let's jump into our discussion.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:13.040
I'd love to open things up to

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:15.040
our audience as well. So if you're in the audience

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:17.040
listening in, then please go ahead.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:19.040
You can submit your questions in the Q&A

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:21.040
widget, and we'll have them read aloud

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:23.040
for Rich and our panel to discuss.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:25.040
In the meantime, while those questions

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:27.040
are coming in, I'd also love to open

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:29.040
this up to our host panelists here.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:31.040
So if you have any questions from which

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:33.040
Rich has shared, please go ahead.

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:35.040
And James, I see that you have your hand up, so if you want

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:37.040
to kick things off, go ahead.

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:39.040
Let me first

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:41.040
premise this with two pieces of, I think, context.

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:43.040
The first piece of context is

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:45.040
I have recently been teaching myself

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:47.040
the Zig programming language,

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:49.040
and their docs are basically the

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:51.040
worst you have ever seen. They're out

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:53.040
of date, they're all machine-generated, they're

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:55.040
impossible to read through,

00:33:55.040 --> 00:33:57.040
yet I still keep using the language,

00:33:57.040 --> 00:33:59.040
and the docs don't really slow me

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:01.040
down that much, and I just kind of get used to writing

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:03.040
code. The second piece of context is

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:05.040
one thing that has always impressed

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:07.040
me about the work of

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:09.040
two of our colleagues, Tom and Dan,

00:34:09.040 --> 00:34:11.040
is that even for very new projects,

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:13.040
I was always impressed by the volume

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:15.040
of documentation that they would

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:17.040
be able to put out for things like Kaputo

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:19.040
version 0.1 that have docs.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:21.040
My question is,

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:23.040
there's some space in between

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:25.040
this really good docs and

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:27.040
absolutely terrible docs, where the

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:29.040
project is actually successful.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:31.040
How does one avoid

